    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">May 1798</span><span class="rf-text">Bonaparte's armada sails from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1798</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a>'s fleet passes the French convoy in the darkness without knowing it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1798</span><span class="rf-text">Bonaparte lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, and Brueys anchors in Aboukir Bay</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1798</span><span class="rf-text">The British fleet attacks the French at about 18:20 on the 1st and the French flagship Orient explodes at 22:00</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1798</span><span class="rf-text">Fighting resumes on the 2nd and the last French ship of the line destroyed or captured, Timoléon, blows up on the 3rd</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1798</span><span class="rf-text">News of the victory reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a> is made Baron Nelson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-the-nile" data-v="b7c0dbe873" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The French line at anchor, the British closing</b> The French thirteen ships of the line lay at anchor along the edge of the shoals, with the van near Aboukir Island and Orient at the centre. The British fleet came up in the evening, with Swiftsure and Alexander out of sight to the west.</li><li><b>Goliath and Zealous cross the head of the line</b> Foley saw a gap between Guerrier and the shoal and took Goliath through it, and Zealous followed. Orion, Theseus and Audacious also passed inside the line, which was ready to fight only on its seaward side.</li><li><b>The seaward ships anchor and the centre is engaged</b> Vanguard, Minotaur and Defence anchored on the seaward side of the French van at 18:40, which was now caught on both sides. Bellerophon and Majestic passed the melee and went on to the unengaged French centre.</li><li><b>Culloden runs onto the shoal</b> Culloden, the last ship of the British line, sailed too close to Aboukir Island in the growing darkness and stuck fast on the shoal. Her boats, the brig Mutine and Leander could not move her.</li><li><b>Bellerophon drifts out and the last ships join</b> Bellerophon, battered by Orient, cut her anchor cables at 20:20 and drifted away from the fight. Swiftsure and Alexander came up in the dark and opened fire on Orient and Franklin.</li><li><b>The French van surrenders</b> Guerrier, Conquérant and Spartiate struck their colours, and Aquilon was dismasted and its officers surrendered at 21:25. Peuple Souverain had already cut its cable and drifted south, and surrendered in the night.</li><li><b>Fire breaks out on Orient</b> A fire was seen on Orient's lower decks and Swiftsure fired into the blaze, which spread through the stern. The nearest British ships edged away, and Tonnant, Heureux and Mercure cut their cables and drifted south.</li><li><b>Orient explodes</b> At 22:00 the fire reached the magazines and Orient was destroyed by a massive explosion. Flaming wreckage fell over the nearest ships, and for ten minutes afterwards there was no firing.</li><li><b>Franklin fights on and surrenders</b> Franklin restarted the engagement by firing on Swiftsure. Isolated and battered, she was dismasted, and the wounded Blanquet surrendered to the combined fire of Swiftsure and Defence.</li><li><b>Tonnant fights on alone and drifts south</b> By midnight only Tonnant remained engaged, fighting Majestic until about 03:00, when she was a dismasted hulk. She then drifted southwards away from the action towards Villeneuve's division.</li><li><b>Firing begins again at dawn</b> As the sun rose, firing broke out again between the French southern division and the battered Alexander and Majestic.</li><li><b>Heureux and Mercure surrender on the shoal</b> Heureux and Mercure had cut their cables to escape the exploding Orient and had drifted onto the shoal. Alexander and three other British ships attacked them, and both surrendered within minutes.</li><li><b>Tonnant is driven ashore and the French rear stands out</b> Villeneuve brought most of the surviving French ships to the mouth of the bay at 11:00. The dismasted Tonnant could not make the speed and was driven ashore by her crew, while the others stood out to sea.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>'s victories in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> helped secure French victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-first-coalition">War of the First Coalition</a> in 1797, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> remained the only major European power still at war with the French Republic. The French navy was dominant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> after the British fleet withdrew following the outbreak of war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1796. Bonaparte proposed invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, nominally part of the neutral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, as a staging point for operations against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. The Directory agreed.</p><p>In spring 1798 Bonaparte assembled more than 35,000 soldiers and a fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a>, and kept the destination secret. The armada sailed on 19 May, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> between 9 and 12 June, and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> on 19 June. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a>, a rear-admiral, was sent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a> by Earl St. Vincent to investigate. He had ten ships of the line and a fourth-rate under Captain Thomas Troubridge by 7 June, but no intelligence of the French destination and no frigates. Having decided that Egypt was the target, he passed the slow French convoy in darkness on 22 June, reached Alexandria on 28 June, found nothing and turned back. French scouts arrived there on the evening of 29 June.</p><p>Bonaparte landed and took Alexandria. His naval commander, Vice-admiral Brueys, found the harbour channel too shallow and narrow for the larger ships and chose Aboukir Bay. Nelson learned of the landing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a> on 28 July and turned south, and on the afternoon of 1 August his scouts sighted the French transports at Alexandria.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson.jpg" alt="Horatio Nelson" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Horatio Nelson</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HoratioNelson1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lemuel Francis Abbott, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Each fleet had 13 ships of the line, but most historians conclude that the French were marginally more powerful. Culloden ran aground, Orient was much larger than Leander, the 50-gun ship of the British force, and the French had two frigates and several smaller vessels in the action. Spartiate, Franklin, Orient, Tonnant and Guillaume Tell were each significantly larger than any single British ship. Brueys flew his flag in the 120-gun Orient at the centre of the line, Counter-admiral Armand Blanquet was in Franklin, and Counter-admiral Pierre-Charles Villeneuve commanded the rear division in Guillaume Tell.</p><p>Brueys anchored his 13 ships of the line along the north-eastern edge of the shoals, 160 yards (150 m) apart, the line 2,850 yards (2,610 m) long, with four frigates in an inner line. He hoped the shoals would force the British to attack his strong centre and rear while his vanguard used the north-easterly wind to counterattack. He left room between the leading ship, Guerrier, and the shoal for an enemy ship to cross the head of the line, and the French prepared only their seaward guns: on the port side the gun ports were closed and the decks uncleared.</p><p>The fleet was short of food and water, and up to a third of the sailors were ashore foraging at any time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a>'s plan was to pass down the seaward side of the French van and centre so that each French ship would face two British ships and Orient three. Each British ship was to show four horizontal lights and a White Ensign, so that ships would not fire on one another in the dark.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At 14:00 on 1 August, lookouts on Zealous reported the French in Aboukir Bay. Brueys had no scouts out and recalled his shore parties, though most had not returned when the battle began. He ordered the line to set sail, then cancelled the order when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a> slowed his leading ships at 16:00 to rig springs on their anchor cables. The British had no charts of the bay beyond a rough sketch and two inaccurate maps. At 18:20 the leading French ships Guerrier and Conquérant opened fire on Goliath and Zealous.</p><p>Captain Thomas Foley in Goliath noticed the gap between Guerrier and the shoal and, on his own initiative, crossed the head of the line, raking Guerrier down its unprepared port side. Hood in Zealous anchored beside Guerrier, whose foremast fell within five minutes. Orion, Theseus and Audacious also crossed or passed inside the line, and Vanguard, Minotaur and Defence anchored on the seaward side at 18:40. Culloden ran onto the shoal in the growing darkness. Guerrier and Spartiate surrendered at 21:00. At about 20:30 Nelson was struck over his blinded right eye by a splinter and carried below, but the surgeon found a flesh wound.</p><p>Bellerophon, under the main battery of Orient, lost her masts and cut her cables at 20:20. Brueys was struck by a cannonball and died fifteen minutes later. At 21:00 a fire was seen on Orient's lower decks, and at 22:00 it reached the magazines and the ship was destroyed by an explosion. Franklin resumed firing after a ten-minute lull and was forced to surrender. By midnight only Tonnant remained engaged.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Firing broke out again at 04:00 on 2 August between the French southern division and the British ships Alexander and Majestic, soon joined by Goliath and Theseus. Heureux and Mercure, which had cut their cables to escape the explosion of Orient, drifted onto the shoal and surrendered within minutes. Guillaume Tell, Généreux, Justice and Diane stood out to sea, pursued by Zealous. On 3 August Tonnant surrendered, with 1,600 survivors from other French vessels on board, and Timoléon was set on fire by its crew and exploded shortly after midday, the eleventh and final French ship of the line destroyed or captured.</p><p>British casualties were recorded as 218 killed and approximately 677 wounded, though the number of wounded who later died is not known. French casualties are harder to calculate; estimates range from 2,000 to 5,000, with a suggested median of 3,500, including more than 1,000 captured and wounded and nearly 2,000 killed, half of them on Orient. Brueys was killed, Blanquet wounded, and four captains died. Two ships of the line and two frigates were destroyed, and three captured ships were too battered ever to sail again.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a> stayed at anchor for two weeks, recovering from his head wound. The first message reached Bonaparte on 14 August, and he later blamed the wounded Blanquet, falsely accusing him of surrendering Franklin while his ship was undamaged. Nelson's first dispatches were lost when Leander was defeated by Généreux off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> on 18 August, so news reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> only on 2 October.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/george-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-iii.jpg" alt="George III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allan_Ramsay_-_King_George_III_in_coronation_robes_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Allan Ramsay, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known from the despatches, letters and memoirs of its participants and from later histories. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a>'s letters record his search for the French. Brueys wrote to the Minister of Marine about the weakness of his crews and rigging, and Seaman John Nicol of Goliath described the bay the morning after, covered with dead bodies. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a>'s speech to Parliament is recorded in William James's naval history of 1827, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s later comment on Villeneuve from his Mémoires. Two accounts of how Bonaparte took the news differ: one has him read the report without emotion, and Bourienne, his secretary, has him almost overcome.</p><p>Archaeology adds to the written record. In 1983 a French survey team under Jacques Dumas found the wreck of Orient, and in 1998 Franck Goddio led a major project in the bay, finding material scattered over 500 metres and evidence of two explosions. He recovered coins from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, probably part of the treasure taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>. In 2000 Paolo Gallo's excavation on Nelson's Island found graves dating from the battle.</p><p>How the fire on Orient began has never been firmly established. One common account is that jars of oil and paint left on the poop deck were ignited by burning wadding from a British ship; Fleet Captain Honoré Ganteaume reported instead an explosion on the quarterdeck, preceded by minor fires among the boats. French losses survive only as a range, and historians debate the comparative strength of the fleets.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/selim-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/selim-iii.jpg" alt="Selim III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/selim-iii">Selim III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konstantin_Kapidagli_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Konstantin Kapıdağlı, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle reversed the strategic situation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> returned in force and blockaded French and allied ports, and the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> lasted two years before the French defenders were starved into surrender. British ships played a crucial part in Bonaparte's defeat at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in 1799, after which he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> without his army, leaving Kléber in command of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> was encouraged to go to war with France, and the Austrian and Russian empires declared war in 1799 as part of a Second Coalition. A British expeditionary force defeated the remains of the French army in Egypt in 1801.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a> was made Baron Nelson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and Burnham Thorpe, a title with which he was privately dissatisfied. Each captain received a gold medal, and the Ottoman Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-iii">Selim III</a> made him a Knight Commander of the new Order of the Crescent. On 29 September 1798 he described his officers as "We few, we happy few, we band of brothers", from which grew the idea of the Nelsonic Band of Brothers. Some have called the battle "arguably, the most decisive naval engagement of the great age of sail". Edward Ingram noted that the campaign has sometimes been considered a strategic success for France, since Bonaparte was free to continue the war in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>.</p><p>The battle is remembered in cartoons, paintings, poems and plays, among them Felicia Hemans's poem Casabianca of 1826, about the death of the captain's son on Orient. Cleopatra's Needle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a> in 1819, was raised on the Victoria Embankment in 1878.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Between 1 and 3 August 1798 a British fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a> attacked the French fleet anchored in Aboukir Bay, killed its commander Brueys, and destroyed or captured 11 of its 13 ships of the line. The defeat left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> cut off and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</em></p>

